This case runs IN PLACE, inside the checkout, on purpose. Do not "fix" it.

Every other case here is copied outside the repository first, so the gate sees
no git and takes the path CI takes. That isolation is the fix for the attachment
coupling - but an isolated fixture can DRIFT from how the gate really runs, and
the failure it would then hide is a false GREEN, which is strictly worse than
the red the isolation removed.

So one case stays on the real path deliberately, and it is a BAD case on
purpose. Only GOOD cases were ever contaminated by attachment: a failing gate
step short-circuits before the counter-model check, so a BAD case reds correctly
in place. That makes this the one case that can hold the real path without
re-importing the defect.

What it protects: if the gate ever stops refusing when run inside a real
repository - because the isolation drifted, or because someone "simplified" the
runner to always isolate - this case goes from BAD to something else, and the
control reds. The isolated cases cannot notice that, by construction.
